
# ============================================================================
# Merlin ontology - top-level index. The `using` lines below pull in the
# kind taxonomy + relation taxonomy that make up the entire ontology.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brief overview
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns are objects or concepts.
#
# States represent something you HAVE or that you ARE.  Unless already
# declared in spatial or attr declarations, they must be declared in states.mon.
#
# A noun is a kind and nothing else. A state that shares a noun's name
# (spouse, gender, job, ..) is a relation in its own right and is declared in
# states.mon with its own fields; a clause whose label is a noun with no such
# declaration is a load error.
#
# Acts (tasks and actions) are things an NPC DOES.  They are NOT declared
# in .mon files; instead they are declared in rules:
# * npc-action rules implicitly declares the action-label as an ontological action.
# * npc-task rules implicitly declares the task-label as an ontological task.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Construals are tags, not beliefs
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The appraisal cascade categorises a task by reading the `construed-act`
# tags on the task's declaration (e.g.
# `frame ... (construed-act harm-act betray-act wrong-act)`) at the anchor's
# task-label. It does NOT mint construal beliefs. Anchors carry their tags
# via the relation_def lookup; categorize runs one reaction row per tag
# per POV (patient / actor / third_party / sadist). Promote a construal
# to a belief only if RETE alpha-indexing genuinely requires it.
#
# Outcome is a substrate property, not a tag bucket. A failed kill is a
# leaf kill-task whose victim survived; the anchor and its tags are the
# same whether the victim died or lived. Survivor cascades differ
# because more living perceivers run the standard reaction rows, not
# because the anchor's tags changed.
#
# ============================================================================

# Concepts, names, objects, etc.

(using concepts)
(using english-names)
(using irish-names)
(using st-revier-names)
(using things)

# Relations

(using states)
(using tasks)

